\begin{abstract}
We study the Vietoris--Rips persistent homology of the prime numbers viewed as a point cloud on the real line, $P=\{p_1,p_2,\dots\}=\{2,3,5,7,\dots\}\subset\RR$, and determine its barcode completely.

Four groups of results are established. First, a \emph{vanishing theorem}: for an arbitrary point cloud on a line the mod-$2$ first homology of the Rips complex vanishes at every scale, so the prime cloud has no degree-one barcode at all; this is sharp, since an explicit four-point planar configuration carries an essential one-cycle. Second, a \emph{rigidity theorem}: for a cloud on a line the degree-zero barcode is the multiset of consecutive gaps, the Betti curve $b_0(\varepsilon,n)=1+\#\{i<n: g_i>\varepsilon\}$ is a complete invariant of that barcode, and the barcode is $2\delta$-stable under $\delta$-perturbations. Third, an \emph{atomicity theorem} and a consequent refutation: every bar of the prime barcode has length $1$ (once) or an even length $\ge 2$, so the bar-length spectrum is supported on the lattice $\{1\}\cup 2\NN$; the number of bars shorter than $2$ among the first $n$ is exactly $1$, whereas an exponential law of any mean $\mu>0$ predicts $n(1-e^{-2/\mu})\to\infty$ such bars. No exponential (Poisson) law, in particular none with mean $\log x$, describes the raw prime barcode. Fourth, a \emph{dictionary} between arithmetic and topology: the twin-prime counting function is exactly the Betti defect at scale $2$, via $b_0(2,n)+\#\{i<n:g_i=2\}=n$, so the twin prime conjecture is equivalent to the unboundedness of $n-b_0(2,n)$; the same translation puts the bounded-gaps theorem of Zhang and Maynard--Tao in the form ``the scale-$246$ Betti defect is unbounded''; and, contrary to naive expectation, at \emph{every} fixed scale the prime cloud has arbitrarily many connected components.

Numerical evidence to $10^6$ (78\,497 bars, mean length $12.74$, longest bar $114$ starting at $492\,113$, $8\,169$ twin bars, $b_0(2,n)=70\,328$) confirms every identity exactly.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} persistent homology, Vietoris--Rips complex, prime gaps, twin primes, Betti curve, bounded gaps, Cram\'er model.

\section{Introduction}

Persistent homology extracts, from a finite metric space, a multiscale summary of its shape: one inflates each point to a ball of radius $\varepsilon/2$, records the homotopy type of the resulting union (combinatorially, of the Vietoris--Rips complex), and tracks the birth and death of homological features as $\varepsilon$ grows. The output is a \emph{barcode}: a multiset of intervals, one per feature.

The primes furnish a canonical infinite point cloud, and the question of what its barcode looks like is natural, concrete, and --- as it turns out --- completely answerable in degree zero and completely trivial in degree one.

There is a specific set of expectations we test. A widely believed heuristic (Cram\'er's model) asserts that the primes behave, in the large, like a Poisson process of intensity $1/\log x$. In the most naive reading this predicts that the degree-zero bar lengths are exponentially distributed with mean $\log x$; and one might further guess that at some large scale --- say $\varepsilon\sim\log^2 x$ --- one-dimensional holes appear, with the longest encoding the twin prime conjecture. We show that both expectations are false, the second for a dimensional reason and the first for a parity reason, and we identify the surviving arithmetic content: the twin prime conjecture \emph{is} a statement about the degree-zero Betti number, evaluated at scale $2$.

Section~\ref{sec:def} fixes definitions; Section~\ref{sec:h1} proves the degree-one vanishing theorem and its sharpness; Section~\ref{sec:h0} treats the degree-zero barcode, rigidity and stability; Section~\ref{sec:atom} proves atomicity and refutes the exponential law; Section~\ref{sec:dict} develops the arithmetic--topology dictionary; Sections~\ref{sec:algo}--\ref{sec:num} give algorithms and numerics; Sections~\ref{sec:disc}--\ref{sec:fut} discuss and propose future work.

\section{Definitions}\label{sec:def}

Throughout, $p_1=2<p_2=3<p_3=5<\cdots$ enumerates the primes and $g_i$ denotes the $i$-th gap in the $0$-indexed convention $g_0=p_2-p_1=1$, $g_1=p_3-p_2=2$, and so on.

\begin{definition}[Line cloud]
A \emph{line cloud} is a strictly increasing map $p:\NN\to\RR$; its $i$-th \emph{gap} is $g_i=p(i+1)-p(i)>0$. The \emph{prime cloud} is $p(i)=p_{i+1}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Vietoris--Rips complex]
For a finite metric space $(X,d)$ and $\varepsilon\ge0$, the Rips complex $\Rips_\varepsilon(X)$ has as $k$-simplices the $(k+1)$-element subsets of diameter at most $\varepsilon$. It is monotone in $\varepsilon$, so $\{\Rips_\varepsilon(X)\}$ is a filtration.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Barcode, Betti curve]
Over a field $\FF$ the persistent homology $H_k(\Rips_\bullet(X);\FF)$ decomposes into interval modules; the multiset of intervals is the degree-$k$ \emph{barcode}, and the \emph{Betti curve} is $\varepsilon\mapsto\dim_\FF H_k(\Rips_\varepsilon(X);\FF)$. We work with $\FF=\FF_2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Betti curve of a line cloud]\label{def:betti}
For a line cloud $p$, $\varepsilon\in\RR$ and $n\in\NN$,
\[
b_0(p;\varepsilon,n) \;=\; 1+\#\{\, i<n : p(i+1)-p(i)>\varepsilon \,\},
\]
the number of connected components of $\Rips_\varepsilon(\{p(0),\dots,p(n)\})$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:barcode}). We write $b_0(\varepsilon,n)$ for the prime cloud.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Barcode multiset, total persistence, defect]
$\mathcal{B}_n(p)=\{\!\{g_0,\dots,g_{n-1}\}\!\}$ is the degree-zero \emph{barcode multiset}; its \emph{total persistence} is $\sum_{i<n}g_i$. The \emph{merge count} is $M(\varepsilon,n)=\#\{i<n:g_i\le\varepsilon\}$ and the \emph{Betti defect} is $(n+1)-b_0(\varepsilon,n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Chain-level degree one, mod $2$]
Over $\FF_2$ a $1$-chain of a line cloud at scale $\varepsilon$ is a finite set $E$ of ordered pairs $(a,b)$ with $a<b$ and $p(b)-p(a)\le\varepsilon$ (a \emph{Rips edge}), with symmetric difference as addition. The \emph{degree} of a vertex in $E$ is the number of edges of $E$ containing it; $E$ is a \emph{cycle} if all degrees are even. A \emph{Rips triangle} is a triple $a<b<c$ with $p(c)-p(a)\le\varepsilon$, with boundary $\{(a,b),(b,c),(a,c)\}$. The \emph{boundary subgroup} is generated by these, and $H_1=\text{cycles}/\text{boundaries}$.
\end{definition}

\section{Degree one: the prime cloud has no holes}\label{sec:h1}

\begin{lemma}[Umbrella lemma]\label{lem:umbrella}
Let $p$ be a line cloud, $\varepsilon\ge0$, and $u,w\le M$ with $p(M)-p(u)\le\varepsilon$ and $p(M)-p(w)\le\varepsilon$. Then $\{u,w,M\}$ has diameter at most $\varepsilon$; in particular $u,w$ are joined by a Rips edge and, if the three indices are distinct, they span a Rips triangle.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Monotonicity places $p(u),p(w)$ in the interval $[p(M)-\varepsilon,p(M)]$ of length $\varepsilon$, so $|p(u)-p(w)|\le\varepsilon$; the diameter of the triple equals $p(M)-\min(p(u),p(w))\le\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

This is the \emph{indifference-graph} property of the Rips graph of a line cloud: two neighbours of a vertex lying on the same side of it are neighbours of each other.

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing of $H_1$ on a line]\label{thm:vanish}
Let $p$ be a line cloud and $\varepsilon\ge0$. Every $1$-cycle of Rips edges is a sum of boundaries of Rips triangles. Consequently $H_1(\Rips_\varepsilon;\FF_2)=0$ for every $\varepsilon$, and the entire degree-one persistence module of a line cloud vanishes.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Descend on $\Phi(E)=\sum_{(a,b)\in E}b$. Let $E\ne\emptyset$ be a cycle with largest vertex $M$. As $\deg_E(M)$ is even and positive there are distinct edges $(u,M),(w,M)\in E$ with $u,w<M$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:umbrella} the triple spans a Rips triangle $T$. Replacing $E$ by $E\mathbin{\triangle}\partial T$ deletes the two edges at $M$ and toggles the lower edge between $u$ and $w$, changing $\Phi$ by $\pm w-2M<0$; the cycle condition is preserved because every degree of $\partial T$ is even. The measure is a strictly decreasing nonnegative integer, so the process terminates at $\emptyset$ and $E$ equals the sum of the triangle boundaries used. The argument is uniform in $\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No essential prime one-cycle]\label{cor:noessential}
There is no scale at which the prime cloud carries an essential $1$-cycle. The conjectured degree-one prime barcode is empty, and no arithmetic information --- in particular not the twin prime conjecture --- can be stored in degree one.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness in dimension one]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $d$ be the graph metric of the $4$-cycle on $\{0,1,2,3\}$ ($d=1$ on adjacent pairs, $d(0,2)=d(1,3)=2$, all further points at distance $100$). At scale $\varepsilon=1$ the Rips complex has edges $\{01,12,23,03\}$ and no $2$-simplex, since every triple of the four vertices contains an antipodal pair at distance $2$. The boundary subgroup is therefore trivial while the square is a cycle, so it represents a nonzero class in $H_1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If a scale admits no $2$-simplex, the boundary subgroup is $\{\emptyset\}$; the square is a nonempty cycle (every vertex has degree $2$), hence not a boundary. The absence of triangles quantifies over all triples, using the distance-$100$ separation outside the square and a finite case analysis inside.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The umbrella lemma fails for the square: the neighbours $1$ and $3$ of the vertex $0$ are at distance $2$. One dimension is exactly the boundary between trivial and nontrivial $H_1$, and the primes lie on the trivial side.
\end{remark}

\section{Degree zero: the barcode is the gap sequence}\label{sec:h0}

\begin{proposition}[Barcode of a line cloud]\label{prop:barcode}
Let $p$ be a line cloud. Two consecutive points lie in the same component of $\Rips_\varepsilon(\{p(0),\dots,p(n)\})$ iff $g_i\le\varepsilon$, components are maximal runs of consecutive indices with all internal gaps $\le\varepsilon$, and hence $b_0(p;\varepsilon,n)=1+\#\{i<n:g_i>\varepsilon\}$. The class of the component ending at index $i$ dies exactly at $\varepsilon=g_i$, so the degree-zero barcode is $\{\!\{[0,g_i): i<n\}\!\}\cup\{[0,\infty)\}$ and the total persistence telescopes:
\[
\sum_{i<n} g_i \;=\; p(n)-p(0).
\]
For the primes the total persistence of the first $n$ bars is $p_{n+1}-2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By monotonicity, $p(j)-p(i)\le\varepsilon$ with $i<j$ forces every intermediate gap to be $\le\varepsilon$, so connectivity is generated by consecutive edges; the component count is one more than the number of surviving breaks, and the elder rule kills the younger component when a break closes.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Defect counts merges]\label{prop:defect}
$b_0(\varepsilon,n)\le n+1$ and $(n+1)-b_0(\varepsilon,n)=M(\varepsilon,n)=\#\{i<n:g_i\le\varepsilon\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity]
For fixed $n$, $\varepsilon\mapsto b_0(p;\varepsilon,n)$ is antitone.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Tail counts determine a finite multiset]\label{thm:tail}
Let $A,B$ be finite multisets of reals with $\#\{a\in A: a>\varepsilon\}=\#\{b\in B:b>\varepsilon\}$ for all $\varepsilon\in\RR$. Then $A=B$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $|A|$. Taking $\varepsilon$ below all elements gives $|A|=|B|$. If both are nonempty, let $a=\max A$, $b=\max B$; if $a<b$, evaluating at $\varepsilon\in[a,b)$ gives $0\ge1$, a contradiction, and symmetrically, so $a=b$. Removing the common maximum transports the hypothesis to the smaller multisets and induction closes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The Betti curve is a complete invariant]\label{thm:complete}
For line clouds $p,q$ and $n\in\NN$,
\[
\big(\forall\varepsilon\; b_0(p;\varepsilon,n)=b_0(q;\varepsilon,n)\big)\iff \mathcal{B}_n(p)=\mathcal{B}_n(q).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Definition~\ref{def:betti}, $b_0(p;\varepsilon,n)$ is $1$ plus the upper-tail counting function of $\mathcal{B}_n(p)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:tail}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Window counts]\label{cor:window}
For $\varepsilon_1\le\varepsilon_2$,
\[
b_0(p;\varepsilon_1,n)-b_0(p;\varepsilon_2,n)=\#\{i<n:\varepsilon_1<g_i\le\varepsilon_2\}.
\]
The Betti curve is the cumulative bar-length histogram; bar-length counts are its discrete derivatives.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Stability / interleaving]\label{thm:stab}
If $|p(i)-q(i)|\le\delta$ for all $i$ then $b_0(q;\varepsilon+2\delta,n)\le b_0(p;\varepsilon,n)$ for all $\varepsilon,n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\varepsilon+2\delta<q(i+1)-q(i)$ then $q(i+1)\le p(i+1)+\delta$ and $q(i)\ge p(i)-\delta$ give $\varepsilon<p(i+1)-p(i)$; the counted index sets are nested.
\end{proof}

\section{Atomicity and the refutation of the exponential law}\label{sec:atom}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:odd}
$g_0=1$; every gap is positive; and every prime after $2$ is odd.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Parity]\label{lem:parity}
For every $i\ge1$, $g_i$ is even.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$g_i$ is the difference of two odd primes by Lemma~\ref{lem:odd}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Atomicity of the bar-length spectrum]\label{thm:atom}
For every index $i$, exactly one of the following holds: $i=0$ and $g_i=1$; or $i\ge1$, $g_i$ is even and $g_i\ge2$. Hence the degree-zero bar-length spectrum of the prime cloud is supported on the lattice $\{1\}\cup2\NN_{\ge1}$, a set of Lebesgue measure zero.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:short}
$g_i<2$ if and only if $i=0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Exactly one short bar]\label{thm:oneshort}
For every $n\ge1$, $\#\{i<n:g_i<2\}=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Refutation of the exponential/Poisson law]\label{thm:refute}
Let $\mu>0$. There is $N\ge1$ such that for all $n\ge N$,
\[
\#\{\,i<n : g_i<2\,\} \;<\; n\big(1-e^{-2/\mu}\big).
\]
An exponential law of mean $\mu$ predicts $n(1-e^{-2/\mu})\to\infty$ bars shorter than $2$, whereas the true count is constantly $1$. Hence no exponential law of any mean --- in particular none with mean $\log x$ --- describes the degree-zero bar lengths of the primes.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $c=1-e^{-2/\mu}$; since $\mu>0$ we have $e^{-2/\mu}<1$, so $c>0$. Choose $N\ge\max(1,\lceil 1/c\rceil+1)$ so that $nc>1$ for $n\ge N$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:oneshort} the left-hand side equals $1<nc$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[What survives]\label{rem:survives}
The refutation concerns the \emph{support} of the barcode measure, not its shape. It cannot be repaired by re-tuning $\mu$: it holds for all $\mu>0$ simultaneously and quantitatively. The correct Cram\'er prediction should be applied to the barcode rescaled by the local mean gap, i.e.\ one should ask whether $n^{-1}\#\{i<n:g_i\le t\log p_i\}\to1-e^{-t}$ for $t>0$, which divides out the lattice while preserving the shape. That statement is open, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:complete} phrasing it in terms of $b_0(t\log x,n)$ loses nothing.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Even-scale rigidity]\label{cor:evenwindow}
For $k\ge1$ and every $n$, $\varepsilon\mapsto b_0(\varepsilon,n)$ is constant on $(2k,2k+2)$: the prime Betti curve jumps only at even scales, and once at scale $1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Atomicity as an arithmetic inequality]\label{cor:pn}
For $n\ge1$, $p_{n+1}\ge2n+1$ and $\sum_{i<n}g_i\ge2n-1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Total persistence telescopes to $p_{n+1}-2$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:atom} the $n$ bars comprise one of length $1$ and $n-1$ of length $\ge2$.
\end{proof}

\section{The arithmetic--topology dictionary}\label{sec:dict}

\subsection{Twin primes as a Betti defect}

Write $T(n)=\#\{i<n:g_i=2\}$ for the number of twin-prime bars.

\begin{lemma}[Three-way split]\label{lem:split}
For $n\ge1$, $\#\{i<n:g_i>2\}+T(n)+1=n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Split $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ by $g_i>2$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:atom} the complement is $\{0\}$ together with the twin indices, and $0$ is not a twin index since $g_0=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The twin prime counting function is a Betti defect]\label{thm:twinbetti}
For every $n\ge1$,
\[
b_0(2,n)+T(n)=n,\qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad T(n)=n-b_0(2,n).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:twindiff}
$T(n)=b_0(1,n)-b_0(2,n)$ for every $n$: the twin-prime count is a single Betti difference.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Twin prime conjecture $=$ unbounded Betti defect]\label{thm:twiniff}
The following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item there are infinitely many twin primes;
\item $\{i:g_i=2\}$ is infinite;
\item for every $K\in\NN$ there is $n$ with $n-b_0(2,n)\ge K$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1)$\Leftrightarrow$(2): a twin pair has no prime strictly between its members, hence is a pair of consecutive primes, i.e.\ a gap $2$, and conversely. (2)$\Rightarrow$(3): extract $K$ indices from the infinite set, all below some $n$; then $T(n)\ge K$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:twinbetti} applies. (3)$\Rightarrow$(2): a finite index set of size $C$ bounds $T(n)\le C$ for all $n$, contradicting $K=C+1$. Subtraction is safe because $b_0(2,n)\le n$ for $n\ge1$.
\end{proof}

Combined with Corollary~\ref{cor:noessential}, the twin prime conjecture lives unambiguously in degree zero: it is a statement about a Betti number at the single fixed scale $2$, in the limit.

\subsection{Bounded gaps as an unbounded defect at a finite scale}

\begin{theorem}[Small bars are early merges]\label{thm:small}
For any $\varepsilon\in\RR$: $\{i:g_i\le\varepsilon\}$ is infinite if and only if the defect $(n+1)-b_0(\varepsilon,n)$ is unbounded in $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The defect equals $M(\varepsilon,n)$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:defect}, and a counting function is unbounded exactly when the counted set is infinite.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Bounded gaps in barcode form]\label{cor:bounded}
Suppose that for every $N$ there exist primes $p<q$ with $p\ge N$ and $q-p\le B$ (the conclusion of the theorems of Zhang and of Maynard--Tao, valid with $B=246$). Then infinitely many prime gaps are at most $B$ and the prime cloud performs arbitrarily many merges at the fixed scale $B$: the scale-$B$ Betti defect is unbounded.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Converse translation]\label{thm:converse}
If the scale-$B$ Betti defect is unbounded, then $\liminf_n (p_{n+1}-p_n)\le B$.
\end{theorem}

Thus the topological and arithmetic formulations of bounded gaps are equivalent, with the twin prime conjecture the case $B=2$.

\subsection{The cloud never connects}

\begin{lemma}[Composite window]\label{lem:window}
For $2\le k\le m$ the number $m!+k$ is composite, since $k\mid m!$ and $m!+k>k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Arbitrarily long bars, arbitrarily late]\label{thm:longbars}
For all $L,N\in\NN$ there is $n\ge N$ with $g_n>L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $m=\max(L+2,\,p_{N+1}+2)$ and let $c$ be the number of primes below $m!+2$; the choice of $m$ gives $c\ge N+1$, so $c-1\ge N$. The $(c-1)$-st prime is at most $m!+1$ and the $c$-th is at least $m!+2$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:window} it is in fact at least $m!+m+1$. Hence $g_{c-1}\ge m>L$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded component count at every scale]\label{thm:unbounded}
For every $\varepsilon\in\RR$ and every $K\in\NN$ there is $n$ with $b_0(\varepsilon,n)\ge K$: at every fixed scale the prime cloud has arbitrarily many connected components and is never eventually connected.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
Theorems~\ref{thm:small} and \ref{thm:unbounded} are two sides of one object: at every fixed scale the prime barcode has infinitely many bars below it and infinitely many above it. The barcode never simplifies.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:algo}

\paragraph{A. Prime barcode.} Sieve to $X$ in $O(X\log\log X)$ time and $O(X)$ bits and emit consecutive differences. By Proposition~\ref{prop:barcode} this \emph{is} the degree-zero barcode; no persistence pairing is needed, which is what makes the computation feasible where general Rips persistence is not (a Rips complex on $78\,497$ points has $\sim3\times10^{9}$ edges).

\paragraph{B. Betti curve and defects.} Sort the bar lengths once in $O(n\log n)$; then $b_0(\varepsilon,n)$ is $1$ plus the number of entries exceeding $\varepsilon$, by binary search in $O(\log n)$. Window counts, twin counts and merge counts are two such queries each.

\paragraph{C. Exponential-law audit.} For each threshold $t$ compare the empirical count $\#\{i<n:g_i\le t\}$ with $n(1-e^{-t/\mu})$. Below $t=2$ the empirical value is pinned at $1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:oneshort}), so the discrepancy is $\Theta(n)$ for every $\mu$; the audit reports the threshold index $n\ge\lceil 1/(1-e^{-2/\mu})\rceil$ at which the prediction first exceeds the truth.

\paragraph{D. Degree-one check.} For a small metric configuration list Rips edges and triangles at scale $\varepsilon$ and compute $\dim H_1=(\#E-\rank\partial_1)-\rank\partial_2$ over $\FF_2$ by Gaussian elimination on bit-vectors. For prime windows the answer is always $0$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:vanish}); for the four-cycle metric it is $1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}).

