The exponential-law audit: with $\mu=12.7391$ the predicted number of bars shorter than $2$ is $\approx11\,405$; with $\mu=\log10^6$ it is $\approx10\,579$; with $\mu=1000$ it is still $\approx157$. The truth is $1$, and for $\mu=12.7391$ the prediction already exceeds $1$ at $n=7$.

The merge identity was verified at $\varepsilon\in\{2,4,6,12,246\}$; at $\varepsilon=2$ both defect and merge count are $8\,170$, and at $\varepsilon=246$ all $78\,497$ bars have merged (no gap below $10^6$ exceeds $114$).

Even-window rigidity at $n=20\,000$: $b_0$ is constant on $(2,4)$ with value $17\,629$, on $(4,6)$ with $15\,275$, on $(6,8)$ with $11\,467$, on $(20,22)$ with $2\,531$; the only odd-scale jump is at $1$, where $b_0$ drops from $20\,001$ to $20\,000$.

Interleaving: perturbing each of the first $5\,001$ primes uniformly in $[-0.4,0.4]$, the bound $b_0^{\text{pert}}(\varepsilon+0.8,n)\le b_0^{\text{prime}}(\varepsilon,n)$ holds at every tested scale.

Degree one: for the primes below $60$, $\dim H_1(\Rips_\varepsilon;\FF_2)=0$ at all tested scales, while the four-cycle metric at scale $1$ has four edges, no triangles and $\dim H_1=1$.

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:disc}

The prime barcode is \emph{topologically rigid} but \emph{statistically non-Poisson}.

Rigid, in three senses: it carries no degree-one information and never can, by a theorem about lines that is sharp at dimension one; its degree-zero content is losslessly encoded by the Betti curve (Theorem~\ref{thm:complete}); and it is stable, so all conclusions concern the \emph{spacing} of the primes rather than accidental features of their positions (Theorem~\ref{thm:stab}).

Non-Poisson, for a structural rather than statistical reason: after the first bar all bars are even, because $2$ is the only even prime, so the barcode measure lives on a lattice. This is worth emphasising because the failure is invisible in coarse statistics --- the empirical mean bar length at $10^6$ is $12.74$ against a predicted $13.82$, agreement good enough to lull one into accepting the model. The obstruction appears only at the resolution of individual bar lengths, which is exactly the resolution at which persistent homology operates.

The positive content is the dictionary: twin primes are a Betti defect at scale $2$; bounded gaps a Betti defect at scale $246$; the elementary bound $p_n\ge2n+1$ is the total-persistence inequality; and the classical composite window becomes the statement that the cloud is never connected. The dictionary is a faithful re-encoding rather than a new arithmetic input, so hard arithmetic remains hard topology. What it provides is a coordinate system in which the difference between what is known and what is conjectured is the value of a single parameter --- the scale at which the defect becomes unbounded.

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:fut}

\paragraph{Rescaled Cram\'er law for the even barcode.} Let $B_n(t)=\#\{i<n:g_i\le t\log p_i\}/n$. Conjecture: $B_n(t)\to1-e^{-t}$ for every $t>0$. The refutation of Section~\ref{sec:atom} concerns the support of the barcode measure, not its shape: dividing each bar by $\log p_i$ destroys the lattice while preserving the shape, so the Poisson prediction should be restated for the rescaled barcode and only then tested. By Theorem~\ref{thm:complete} this is equivalently a statement about the staircase $n^{-1}(n+1-b_0(t\log p_n,n))$; the natural first targets are the conditional lower bound from Hardy--Littlewood and the unconditional upper bound from Brun's sieve.

\paragraph{Higher-dimensional delay embedding.} Embed the primes in $\RR^d$ by $\Phi_d(n)=(p_n,\dots,p_{n+d-1})$. Conjecture: for $d\ge2$ the Rips filtration of $\Phi_d(\NN)$ has nonvanishing $H_1$, with longest bar born at scale $\asymp\log^2x$ --- the scale originally, and wrongly, conjectured for the one-dimensional cloud. Correlations between consecutive gaps, invisible on a line, become geometry in the delay embedding.

\paragraph{Interpolating the small-gaps programme.} Define $B^\ast=\inf\{B:\text{the scale-}B\text{ defect is unbounded}\}$; known $B^\ast\le246$, conjectured $B^\ast=2$. Is there a topological route to lowering $B^\ast$?

\paragraph{Other arithmetic clouds.} The framework applies to any increasing arithmetic sequence --- primes in a residue class, sums of two squares, smooth numbers --- each with its own atomicity signature, Betti staircase and distinguished-scale defects.

\paragraph{Multiparameter persistence.} Filtering by scale and by position yields a two-parameter module whose rank invariant is $b_0(\varepsilon,n)$ in both variables; the rescaled Cram\'er law is then a statement about the fibered barcode along $\varepsilon=t\log x$.

\section{Conclusion}

The primes, viewed as a point cloud on the line, have a shape, and we have determined it. In degree one there is nothing, for a reason that is a theorem about lines and is sharp at dimension one. In degree zero the barcode is the gap sequence; its Betti curve is a complete, stable invariant, constant between consecutive even scales; its bar lengths are pinned to the lattice $\{1\}\cup2\NN$, ruling out the exponential law for every mean; its total persistence is $p_n-2$, whose atomic lower bound is $p_n\ge2n+1$; and its defects at fixed finite scales are the twin prime counting function ($\varepsilon=2$) and the bounded-gaps phenomenon ($\varepsilon=246$). At every scale the cloud shatters into arbitrarily many components.

The primes are not random, and their barcode says so in one line of parity. What remains random-looking about them survives the rescaling by $\log x$ --- and that is where the topology now points.

